me  Minister conveyed at the g7 as well some  of his counterparts which is that we  want any future agreement not to have  the backstop now you know I've read all  the stuff that's gone on today in the  papers nor the rest of it the reality is  that the previous withdrawal agreement  which contained the backstop did not  pass on three occasions didn't pass then  it won't pass again and in fact having  the backstop also potentially makes us  rule takers from the EU for forever that  is not what we want we want that out we  want a deal but we will be leaving on  the 31st of October no ifs no buts this  is where it gets complicated and where I  think people at home might start to tear  their hair out you say you want a deal  you want the backstop out that's  absolutely crucial to that deal Michel  Barnier  and indeed the rest of the EU  negotiating team on this say that is not  going to happen  absolutely not on the table why don't  you just therefore come out and say  actually we're just gunning for a no  deal now well we've said we want a deal  and actually you talk about the EU it  takes two to tango what we are doing  very clearly is setting out the case  that we won't have a deal we've set out  the parameters for that and we've said  we're prepared to have that discussion  and the Prime Minister had some good  discussions at the g7  but what we're also doing is preparing  for the eventualities off a No Deal  we've turbocharged the work that we're  doing there so we are ready and the best  way Steven the Prime Minister made this  very clear the best way of making sure  we get a deal is to demonstrate that we  are ready for No Deal  but again it's back to that point of I  mean do we believe what politicians are  saying and say we're really pushing for  a deal here but when we're told  categorically what you were asking for  will not happen  why can't politicians then turn around  and say well actually it's we know we're  not gonna get this so we're going for a  No D